//? Title: xoroshiro128+: Balanced Bits Can Still Be Predictable
//? Subtitle: A reversible two-word generator, held-out recurrence checks and a car-data bootstrap
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
xoroshiro128+ combines a 128-bit XOR/rotate transition with addition of two
64-bit words. We derive the transition's inverse and show why its lowest
output bit remains linear despite the addition scrambler. A recurrence fitted
on an initial segment predicts a separate suffix, revealing a weakness that
bit frequencies and a small goodness-of-fit battery can miss. The generator
then drives an ordinary bootstrap of mean fuel economy in
`dm::datasets::mtcars()`, checked against the exact conditional standard error.
Published jump operations organise reproducible simulation streams without
turning disjoint state ranges into a claim of independence.
)

\toc

> The data: fuel economy in a selected set of cars

The built-in `mtcars` dataset contains 32 cars and 11 numeric variables.
Fuel economy `mpg` is in miles per US gallon and weight `wt` is in thousands
of pounds. The cars have different cylinder counts, weights and engines;
they are a selected historical set, not a random sample of today's fleet.
Our resampling calculation describes the empirical distribution of these
records under an exchangeable-record model. It does not estimate a causal
weight effect or automatically generalise to other cars.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/xoroshiro_cars.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto cars = dm::datasets::mtcars();
  std::print("{}\n", cars.select({"mpg", "cyl", "wt", "hp"}).head(6).to_md());
  std::print("\n{}\n", (cars | dm::group_by("cyl") |
      dm::summarise(dm::agg::n("cars"), dm::agg::mean("mpg", "mean_mpg"),
          dm::agg::sd("mpg", "sd_mpg"), dm::agg::mean("wt", "mean_wt"))).to_md(4));
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create().points(cars.get_column("wt").values(), cars.get_column("mpg").values(),
      "cars", dm::RGB{37,99,235}, 4)
      .title("Fuel economy and weight in 32 historical cars")
      .x_label("weight (1000 lb)").y_label("fuel economy (miles per US gallon)")
      .size(900,480).save_svg("build/xoroshiro_cars.svg");
}
```
