> The method: addition hides some linear structure, but not the lowest bit

For old state words \((a,b)\), put \(q=a\oplus b\). Version 1.0
uses the 24/16/37 parameters, rather than the earlier 55/14/36 variant.
With all words truncated to 64 bits, output and simultaneous transition are

$$
y=a+b\pmod{2^{64}},\qquad
T(a,b)=\left(\operatorname{rotl}(a,24)\oplus q\oplus(q\ll16),\
\operatorname{rotl}(q,37)\right).
$$
\alttext(The output adds the old words modulo two to the sixty-four. The next state XORs a rotated first word with the XOR of the old words and its shifted copy, then rotates that XOR for the second word.)

XOR, fixed shifts and rotations are linear over \(\mathbf F_2\), so
this is a binary linear state transition. Given a next state \((u,v)\),
its inverse follows directly:

$$
q=\operatorname{rotr}(v,37),\qquad
a=\operatorname{rotr}\left(u\oplus q\oplus(q\ll16),24\right),\qquad
b=q\oplus a.
$$
\alttext(Undo the second rotation to recover the XOR of the old words, then undo the first rotation to recover the first word and XOR to recover the second.)

This proves reversibility. The stronger claim that all nonzero states lie
on one cycle of length \(2^{128}-1\) comes from the published primitive
characteristic polynomial, not from the inverse or a short empirical run.
The zero state is absorbing and rejected by the direct-state constructor.
A scalar seed fills two words from successive SplitMix64 outputs. Those
outputs are distinct because SplitMix's counter step and scrambling are
invertible, so the pair cannot be all zero.

Addition is nonlinear over XOR because of carries, but there is no carry
into bit zero. Thus the lowest output bit is exactly

$$
z_t=(a_t)_0\oplus(b_t)_0=\ell(T^t s_0),\qquad
z_t=\bigoplus_{j=1}^{L}c_j z_{t-j},\quad L\le128.
$$
\alttext(The lowest output bit is the XOR of the lowest state bits, a linear functional of the 128-bit state. Cayley–Hamilton gives it a binary recurrence of length at most 128.)

The recurrence bound follows from applying the degree-128 characteristic
identity for \(T\) to the state and then the linear functional \(\ell\).
A sequence can have almost half ones and still obey such a short recurrence.
The authors explicitly identify low linear complexity in the lowest bits;
`uniform01()` instead uses the top 53 bits of a raw output.

> Exact state and jump checks

The published jump polynomials evaluate \(T^{2^{64}}\) and
\(T^{2^{96}}\) using XOR accumulation over 128 transitions. They apply
to the 24/16/37 version specifically. The reference states below are
checked against the authors' compiled C implementation, including eight
outputs after each starting point.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::print("| position | s0 | s1 | next output |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n");
  for (int mode : {0,1,2}) {
    dm::random::Xoroshiro128Plus rng(std::array<std::uint64_t,2>{1,2});
    if (mode == 1) rng.jump();
    if (mode == 2) rng.long_jump();
    const auto state = rng.state();
    std::print("| {} | {:016x} | {:016x} | {:016x} |\n",
        mode == 0 ? "initial" : mode == 1 ? "2^64 jump" : "2^96 jump", state[0], state[1], rng.next_u64());
  }
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| position | s0 | s1 | next output |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| initial | 0000000000000001 | 0000000000000002 | 0000000000000003 |
| 2^64 jump | 66fbd4be1df0a7b5 | 830c3ddbb4aa3172 | ea081299d29ad927 |
| 2^96 jump | 3ce44494d47d323a | 2aa25ca8d61de643 | 6786a13daa9b187d |
// result_end
\caption(Raw states and outputs in hexadecimal, avoiding loss of integer precision through floating-point table columns.)

State snapshots exclude any second normal variate cached by the distribution
interface. Jumps clear that cache, while copying a complete engine preserves
both the raw state and the cache. Disjoint state ranges require stream
lengths smaller than the jump spacing and no wrap around the full period;
they are not a proof of statistical independence.

> A recurrence trained on one segment and checked on another

`bit_linear_complexities()` uses the existing Berlekamp–Massey solver on
a prefix, fixes its coefficients, and predicts each held-out bit using the
preceding observed bits. It never refits on the suffix. Here there are
512 training words and 512 validation words, with six bit positions chosen
before inspection. The one-frequency column uses the full 1024 words. When multiple shortest
recurrences fit a prefix, the solver retains its deterministic choice.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/xoroshiro_prediction.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  dm::random::Xoroshiro128Plus rng(42);
  std::vector<std::uint64_t> words(1024);
  for (auto& word : words) word = rng.next_u64();
  const auto diagnostic = dm::random::bit_linear_complexities(words,512);
  std::print("{}\n", diagnostic.to_md(5));
  std::vector<std::string> labels{"0","1","2","3","31","63"};
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(diagnostic.get_column("error_rate").values(),labels,"error fraction")
      .abline_h(0.5,dm::RGB{220,38,38},2)
      .title("A balanced lowest bit is predictable on unseen outputs")
      .x_label("output bit position").y_label("held-out prediction error fraction")
      .y_limits(0,0.6).size(900,480).save_svg("build/xoroshiro_prediction.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| bit | training_size | heldout_size | linear_complexity | heldout_errors | error_rate | one_frequency |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 512 | 512 | 128 | 0 | 0.00000 | 0.50195 |
| 1 | 512 | 512 | 258 | 264 | 0.51562 | 0.50488 |
| 2 | 512 | 512 | 256 | 247 | 0.48242 | 0.50586 |
| 3 | 512 | 512 | 257 | 239 | 0.46680 | 0.53711 |
| 31 | 512 | 512 | 257 | 238 | 0.46484 | 0.51660 |
| 63 | 512 | 512 | 256 | 272 | 0.53125 | 0.52148 |

\image(figures/xoroshiro_prediction.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Training-prefix recurrence lengths and suffix prediction errors. The red line is the ideal independent-bit error fraction, one half.)
\alttext(A bar chart with zero prediction error for bit zero and error fractions near one half for the other five selected bit positions.)

For an ideal independent fair-bit stream, any predictor fixed from the
training segment and based on previous observations makes an error with
conditional probability one half on each next bit. A zero-error suffix of
512 bits would have probability \(2^{-512}\) for that fixed diagnostic.
This is a structural demonstration of the low-bit limitation, not a general
ranking of generators. Larger samples and different diagnostics can expose
other weaknesses; high bits are not made cryptographically secure by passing.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/xoroshiro_complexity.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  dm::random::Xoroshiro128Plus rng(42);
  std::vector<std::uint64_t> words(2048);
  for (auto& word : words) word = rng.next_u64();
  std::vector<double> n, low, high, reference;
  std::print("| training words | lowest-bit L | highest-bit L |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const std::size_t size : {128,256,512,1024}) {
    const auto row = dm::random::bit_linear_complexities(words,size,{0,63});
    n.push_back(size); low.push_back(row.double_at("linear_complexity",0));
    high.push_back(row.double_at("linear_complexity",1)); reference.push_back(size/2.0);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n",size,low.back(),high.back());
  }
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create().line(n,low,"bit 0",dm::RGB{220,38,38},2)
      .line(n,high,"bit 63",dm::RGB{37,99,235},2)
      .line(n,reference,"N / 2",dm::RGB{100,100,100},1)
      .title("Low-bit linear complexity stops growing at 128")
      .x_label("training-prefix length N").y_label("shortest binary recurrence length")
      .size(900,480).save_svg("build/xoroshiro_complexity.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| training words | lowest-bit L | highest-bit L |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 128 | 64 | 63 |
| 256 | 128 | 128 |
| 512 | 128 | 256 |
| 1024 | 128 | 512 |
\image(figures/xoroshiro_complexity.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The lowest-bit recurrence reaches length 128; the highest-bit finite-prefix complexity grows near the independent-bit scale N/2 over this range.)
\alttext(A line chart showing lowest-bit complexity levelling at 128, while highest-bit complexity rises with roughly half the training length.)

> A small quality battery addresses different questions

The seven-test battery measures marginal uniformity, serial pairs, lag-one
correlation, raw monobit balance and low-byte frequencies. It contains no
linear-complexity test. Forty fresh scalar-seeded engines provide a
second-level KS check of each test's p-values, with 100000 observations per
first-level test. Approximate reference distributions, discrete statistics,
multiple testing and related deterministic seed choices limit interpretation.
All seven results are retained, without selecting a favourable seed.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto tests = dm::random::quality_tests_over_seeds([](std::uint64_t seed) {
    return std::make_unique<dm::random::Xoroshiro128Plus>(seed);
  },40,100000);
  std::print("{}\n",tests.select({"test","mean_p","small_p_count","large_p_count","ks_p_value"}).to_md(5));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| test | mean_p | small_p_count | large_p_count | ks_p_value |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Uniformity, chi-square (100 cells) | 0.56627 | 3 | 4 | 0.11277 |
| Uniformity, Kolmogorov-Smirnov | 0.40402 | 2 | 1 | 0.06447 |
| Serial pairs, chi-square (10 x 10 cells) | 0.50785 | 3 | 4 | 0.80132 |
| Lag-1 correlation, z = r sqrt(n) | 0.50358 | 1 | 3 | 0.94221 |
| Monobit, lowest bit (z) | 0.43006 | 3 | 0 | 0.17553 |
| Monobit, highest bit (z) | 0.50801 | 2 | 5 | 0.69888 |
| Lowest byte, chi-square (256 cells) | 0.44831 | 1 | 0 | 0.29535 |
// result_end
\caption(Per-test summaries across seeds 1–40. Under ideal calibration about two first-level p-values below 0.05 and two above 0.95 are expected per row.)

> Applying the engine: resampling mean fuel economy

For observations \(x_1,\ldots,x_n\), let \(s^2\) be their sample
variance with denominator \(n-1\). An ordinary bootstrap draws \(n\)
records independently with replacement from the empirical distribution.
Its single-draw variance and exact conditional mean variance are

$$
\operatorname{Var}_*(X^*)=\frac{n-1}{n}s^2,\qquad
\operatorname{Var}_*(\bar X^*)=\frac{n-1}{n^2}s^2,\qquad
\operatorname{SE}_*(\bar X^*)=s\frac{\sqrt{n-1}}{n}.
$$
\alttext(The empirical-distribution variance is n minus one over n times the sample variance. Dividing by n yields the exact bootstrap mean variance.)

The caller-owned generator controls the random draws; the seed stored in
`BootstrapOptions` is ignored by this overload. Indices use unbiased
multiply-and-reject bounded sampling, not a raw word modulo \(n\).
For the 32-record sample, this uses the top five bits with no rejection,
so the demonstrated raw lowest-bit recurrence does not choose the indices.
This claim is specific to \(n=32\); for other bounds the multiplication
and rejection rule must be considered directly.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/xoroshiro_bootstrap.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto mpg = dm::datasets::mtcars().get_column("mpg").values();
  dm::random::Xoroshiro128Plus rng(2026);
  const auto result = dm::stats::Bootstrap({10000,0.95,42}).run(mpg,dm::mean,rng);
  const double n = mpg.size(), exact = dm::sd(mpg)*std::sqrt(n-1)/n;
  std::print("| cars | mean mpg | exact conditional SE | simulated SE | bias | percentile lower | percentile upper |\n"
      "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n"
      "| {} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} |\n",mpg.size(),result.estimate,exact,
      result.standard_error,result.bias,result.percentile_interval.first,result.percentile_interval.second);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RPlot::hist(result.replicates,35,"bootstrap means")
      .abline_v(result.estimate,dm::RGB{220,38,38},2)
      .title("Mean fuel economy: 10000 empirical bootstrap replicates")
      .x_label("bootstrap mean (miles per US gallon)").y_label("replicates")
      .size(900,480).save_svg("build/xoroshiro_bootstrap.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| cars | mean mpg | exact conditional SE | simulated SE | bias | percentile lower | percentile upper |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 32 | 20.090625 | 1.048645 | 1.037189 | -0.022860 | 18.071875 | 22.156328 |
\image(figures/xoroshiro_bootstrap.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The simulated bootstrap standard error beside its exact conditional value. Percentile endpoints describe this exchangeable-record resampling model.)
\alttext(A near-bell-shaped histogram of resampled mean fuel economy, centred near 20 miles per US gallon, with the original mean marked.)

The simulated SE is 1.03719 mpg, about 1.1% below the exact 1.04865 mpg.
For approximately normal replicates, the rough Monte Carlo SE of this
standard-deviation estimate is \(1.04865/\sqrt{2(10000-1)}\), about 0.00742 mpg.
The difference is about 1.5 such Monte Carlo standard errors. Independent
Python reproduces all 10000 means, and R agrees on the summary and percentile
endpoints.

Increasing the replication count reduces Monte Carlo noise in these
summaries. It cannot correct the original cars' selection or establish an
independent-observation model. Agreement with the exact conditional variance
checks the resampling calculation, not general statistical quality of the
engine on every application.

> Conclusion

The 24/16/37 transition is reversible, and published jump polynomials match
the authors' reference stream. Addition leaves a linear lowest bit whose
short recurrence predicts new outputs even though balance alone looks
ordinary. A modest quality battery and a held-out recurrence check answer
different questions. In the car bootstrap the bounded-index API uses upper
bits, and an exact conditional variance provides an application-specific
oracle while preserving the limits of the resampling model.

> References

- D. Blackman and S. Vigna (2021). *Scrambled Linear Pseudorandom Number Generators*.
  ACM Transactions on Mathematical Software 47(4). https://doi.org/10.1145/3460772
- Authors' xoroshiro128+ 1.0 reference transition and jump operations,
  https://prng.di.unimi.it/xoroshiro128plus.c
- J. L. Massey (1969). *Shift-Register Synthesis and BCH Decoding*.
  IEEE Transactions on Information Theory 15(1), 122–127. https://doi.org/10.1109/TIT.1969.1054260
- B. Efron (1979). *Bootstrap Methods: Another Look at the Jackknife*.
  Annals of Statistics 7(1), 1–26. https://doi.org/10.1214/aos/1176344552
